+### App Store Screenshot Lane
+
+App Store screenshots are a release asset, not a marketing afterthought.
+
+Required SOP:
+- Capture and validate App Store screenshots on the Mini, not on the Air.
+- Do not run App Store screenshot capture from a plain `ssh mini 'bash ...'` shell. Use the Mini GUI session. The app capture scripts now auto-reroute through `scripts/mini/mini-gui-run.sh` when triggered from SSH on the Mini, because macOS window rendering can hang or produce blank assets without a real GUI-backed Terminal session.
+- Treat Terminal cleanup as part of the screenshot lane, not optional cleanup afterward. The shared Mini runner now tags automation windows with `SaneApps Automation:`, reclaims stale windows automatically, and screenshot lanes must request a full reclaim before they capture.
+- If the Mini desktop already looks polluted, reclaim it first with `ssh mini '~/SaneApps/infra/SaneProcess/scripts/mini/mini-reclaim-automation-windows.sh --all --hide-terminal'`.
+- Use dedicated App Store screenshot assets. Do not reuse undersized website images and let App Store Connect scale them.
+- Audit every screenshot against the real app before upload. Each frame must show a real, current feature state and a clear story:
+  - what the screen is
+  - why the feature matters
+  - why a buyer would care
+- Reject any screenshot that is blurry, off-spec, stale, contaminated by Terminal/browser/dev UI, cropped into nonsense, or showing private/junk/demo text that would confuse a customer.
+- For every app with `appstore.enabled: true`, keep both scripts present and working:
+  - `scripts/capture_appstore_screenshots.sh`
+  - `scripts/validate_appstore_screenshots.sh`
+- For every app with `appstore.enabled: true`, keep `docs/appstore_screenshot_storyboard.yml` current. That storyboard is the source of truth for what each slot is supposed to show, why it matters, and which UI details must be visible.
+- Match Apple’s accepted screenshot sizes exactly. Current common lanes:
+  - iPhone 6.9": `1260x2736`, `1290x2796`, or `1320x2868`
+  - iPad 13": `2064x2752` or `2048x2732`
+  - Mac: `1280x800`, `1440x900`, `2560x1600`, or `2880x1800`
+  - Apple Watch: accepted device-specific sizes only
+- If screenshot text includes pricing or an upgrade CTA, that text must come from the app’s current runtime/display price label, not a hardcoded string in the screenshot view.
+- If an app has `scripts/validate_appstore_screenshots.sh`, `SaneMaster.rb appstore_preflight` now runs it and fails if it reports stale text, wrong sizes, or stale files.
+- `appstore_submit.rb` must also pass the app-level screenshot validator before it uploads screenshots, including `--screenshots-only` runs.
+- Run the shared public-page audit after any screenshot or metadata change that reaches App Store Connect:
+  - `ruby ~/SaneApps/infra/SaneProcess/scripts/appstore_public_screenshot_audit.rb --project-root /path/to/app`
+  - The audit downloads the live customer-facing screenshots, compares them against the storyboard/local files, and writes an HTML plus JSON report.
+  - Open the generated HTML report and inspect the images side by side. Filename/order checks are not enough on their own because a stale live screenshot can keep the same basename while still showing old pixels or old pricing.
+- App Store Connect screenshot sets are effectively immutable once the lane is already submitted and live. If ASC returns `INVALID_STATE` / `Can't Delete Screenshot After Submit for review`, stop. Do not pretend the upload worked. The fix now requires a new App Store version, not another screenshot-only retry.
+- When price, paywall, App Store metadata, or website pricing changes, run a parity audit in this order:
+  1. `.saneprocess` metadata and IAP price
+  2. in-app runtime/display price labels
+  3. regenerated screenshot assets plus validator pass
+  4. App Store Connect IAP price schedule
+  5. live public App Store page price/copy/screenshots
+  6. website/direct-store copy
+- Do not call the rollout complete until the live public App Store page matches the canonical price and the submitted screenshots are sharp, current, and accurate.
+
+Practical rule:
+- upload-time resizing is not a fix. If the repo asset is off-spec, blurry, stale, or showing the wrong price, fix the source asset and regenerate it before submission.
+- If the screenshot is accurate but weak, replace it. “Technically true” is not good enough if it fails to explain the product or why someone should buy it.
+- Screenshot QA happens before submission, not after. If you discover a bad live screenshot on a `READY_FOR_SALE` lane, treat it as a release blocker for the next version and queue the corrected assets immediately.
+- Do not trust filename globs alone. The storyboard, the validator, and the live public audit all need to agree before an App Store screenshot lane is considered healthy.
+
+Use this order:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/capture_appstore_screenshots.sh
+ruby scripts/SaneMaster.rb appstore_preflight
+ruby scripts/appstore_submit.rb --screenshots-only --app-id <APP_ID> --project-root .
+ruby scripts/appstore_submit.rb --sync-metadata-only --app-id <APP_ID> --project-root .
+```
+
